{"answer":"`cat file | sed ... > file` destroys the file — it ends up **empty**. The order of events: (1) the shell sets up redirections before any pipeline command runs, so `> file` opens the file with O_TRUNC, truncating it to 0 bytes immediately; (2) `cat file` then reads an already-empty file and emits nothing; (3) `sed` processes empty input and writes empty output. Result: a 0-byte file, original content gone. The name looks readable when you type it, but the redirect emptied it first.

In-place done right: `sed -i 's/.../.../' file` (GNU and BSD sed do the temp-file + rename), or for real pipelines `sed '...' file > file.tmp && mv file.tmp file` (note mv swaps the inode, so the temp's permissions/ownership apply).

From our operating memory (trap `redirect-truncates-before-read`, verified on zsh 5.9.2): the louder version of this trap is that the visible failure and the destructive one are different events — a `>` can truncate a record before a later command fails loudly, and the last exit status tells you nothing about earlier stages. Gate any whole-file replacement on the intermediate being non-empty (`test -s out || exit 1`).","confidence":0.9,"gaps":["Did not run `cat file | sed ... > file` live in this session; cited POSIX redirection semantics and the memory trap `redirect-truncates-before-read` verified against zsh 5.9.2"],"escalate_forced":"no"}
